WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.250
You can change the world
to meet your values when

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:06.750
you are in government.

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:08.440
You can't when you
are in opposition.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:13.830
Getting into government has to
be the first and sole purpose

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.400
of any political party.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:20.430
I was there in the 1980s when
the hard left held a dominant

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:22.140
position in the Labour party.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:28.080
When Labour had 18, 19 years
of continuous opposition.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.840
And I learned the
hard way really,

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:36.250
that extreme left politics just
didn't connect to the voters.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:40.980
But I was also there in
1997 and through the years

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:42.480
of the Blair-Brown governments.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.380
When I saw what impact
one could have when we

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:47.970
controlled the levers of power.

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:53.280
I am virtually the last Jewish
woman Labour MPs left standing.

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:22.250
If we don't learn the
lessons of why we have

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:25.280
lost the 2019 election.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:30.365
If we allow the heir to Corbyn
to be Rebecca Long-Bailey...

00:01:30.365 --> 00:01:35.120
Did you stand up at any
point, and call it out?

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:37.060
I did, but was that enough?

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:37.670
No.

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:38.495
It wasn't.

00:01:38.495 --> 00:01:41.090
The idea that we will ever
get a Labour government,

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:43.940
certainly in my
lifetime, will disappear.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:46.560
I mean, if I had a majority
of 80 Margaret, just think...

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.820
...even just one thing,
you'd be like giddy.

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:51.620
Like a child in a sweet shop.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:54.460
The last four years, we have
to be honest with people,

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:57.080
it's been a fucking disaster
for the Labour party.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:00.090
The last thing I want
is more, more Corbyn.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:02.860
You said last time you
wanted to win, didn't you?

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.390
I've known Corbyn since '82.

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:07.350
If there's one thing
he really believes in,

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:10.699
it's all that massive
world view, which

00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:13.910
is hostile to Nato,
hostile to the States,

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:16.150
leads to the anti-Semitism.

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:16.650
Rebecca.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:19.685
We're going to take ....
once Adrian's finished.

00:02:19.685 --> 00:02:21.250
I didn't break rank
and tell people

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:22.470
what's going on in
the shadow cabinet,

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:23.928
on this or anything
else, because I

00:02:23.928 --> 00:02:24.980
don't think you should.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.960
I consider my position
in the Labour party,

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:28.627
over the last few
years, to be honest...

00:02:28.627 --> 00:02:30.860
I put myself
forward, for frankly,

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:34.790
the worst job in the world,
which is being leader

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:36.740
of the Labour party when
we're in opposition.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.937
For me, I'm going
for Jess Phillips.

00:02:46.937 --> 00:02:48.520
When I've seen the
others against her,

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:50.815
a lot of sort of
traditional politicians.

00:02:50.815 --> 00:02:53.510
We need someone who can
stand opposite Boris Johnson,

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:56.860
and wear his
government in his face.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:01.630
The Labour leadership race
is narrowing from 5 to 4.

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:04.420
This time, that person isn't me.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:05.815
Oh.

00:03:05.815 --> 00:03:08.027
So, Jess was due to come
and speak at the event.

00:03:08.027 --> 00:03:08.282
Yeah.

00:03:08.282 --> 00:03:10.282
But she's gone back to
Birmingham, so she can't.

00:03:10.282 --> 00:03:10.810
Yeah.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:13.270
We've sent her some flowers.

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:15.520
What could have been?

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:18.350
Oh goodness, that
is really hard.

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:23.990
I mean, I watched Jess's
video, and it's really moving,

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:29.080
and I wanted to cry when I
watched that for I think,

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:31.480
what the Labour party's lost.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:35.980
But I'm a sort of, you know,
I'm an old, old fighter,

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:37.170
that's the real truth.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:39.668
So I'm going to
carry on fighting.

00:03:39.668 --> 00:03:41.710
And I just want to see
what the other candidates,

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:43.793
there's three of them
really, that I'm looking at.

00:03:43.793 --> 00:03:50.230
Who can fill the void that
Jess's departure has created.

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:52.380
When I've seen the others
against a lot of the most

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:57.030
sort of traditional politicians,
men in suits quite often.

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:58.905
He's triangulating like mad.

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:00.280
Somebody said to
me, I don't mind

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:03.440
what he does as long as he
wins, beats Rebecca Long-Bailey.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.400
And I thought you know,
Tony never did that.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:11.200
Tony was completely
straight, completely honest.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.390
It's a different way
of doing your politics.

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:15.480
So is Keir lying to get the job?

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:16.480
And will he then change?

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.310
That's what this
person was saying to me

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:19.660
as a way of promoting Keir.

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:20.350
I mean that's...

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:23.400
so I know.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:28.310
So it's sort of, in a
way, you then think, um.

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:30.040
Right.

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:32.100
Who's not got
their seat belt on?

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.830
Those people who chose
to join Momentum,

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:36.960
I think probably a
lot have left now,

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:41.940
are young idealists
for whom at the time

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:43.650
Corbyn was a message of hope.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:46.080
They are the ones who
don't like the politics,

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.300
don't like the
anti-Semitism, and don't

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:49.800
like the anti-Europeanism.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:53.310
You'll start to see this in
who's nominating Keir Starmer.

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:56.600
You know, Momentum is cracking
up, it's sort of you know,

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:59.030
the old Trots will
all go for Rebecca.

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:03.490
As will the out-of-date,
fat bellied, beer bellied,

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:04.650
trade union barons.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:05.760
I don't know where's Owen.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:07.950
Is Owen pro-European?

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:09.210
Probably he is, actually.

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:14.250
I am cross because he
is 40 minutes late.

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:18.100
You know, everybody's
busy, and you just -

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:21.180
if you busy people
make me busy people,

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:22.650
don't piss him off
for being late.

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:24.030
Anyway, we are where we are.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:25.230
Why do I talk to him?

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:29.430
Because Owen is a
Corbyn supporter,

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:33.000
and also he's been a
pretty consistent critic

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:36.660
of the Blair-Brown years,
actually of Labour in power

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:39.150
and in government, and I want
to challenge him a little bit

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:40.410
on that too.

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:41.680
You are very late.

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:44.213
I've been in the Labour
party for a very long time.

00:05:44.213 --> 00:05:45.705
Longer than I've been alive.

00:05:45.705 --> 00:05:46.300
Indeed.

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:49.980
So I remember the 80s, which you
know were very uncomfortable.

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:52.650
Where you used to go along
to Labour party meetings

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:55.260
and if you dare to put up your
hand on something you believed

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:57.670
in - all you were doing
was following your values,

00:05:57.670 --> 00:06:00.750
which may be different,
there was intolerance of it.

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:03.040
People would shout abuse at you.

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:05.880
I had bricks thrown at
me during discussions

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:07.210
on cuts in local government.

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:07.920
I did.

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:09.240
I've had death threats.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:12.270
I've had it all my life, and
I got it from the left then.

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:14.700
The Labour party
then went through,

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:17.620
under the Blair-Brown years,
which you like to dismiss.

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:18.390
I don't dismiss.

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:19.155
That's not true.

00:06:19.155 --> 00:06:19.350
OK.

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:20.350
The Labour party went...

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:22.650
I was a Labour member
under Blair and Brown.

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:23.150
Brilliant.

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:24.192
Well, I'm really pleased.

00:06:24.192 --> 00:06:26.910
So it went through a
really tolerant period,

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:31.527
when you could get up and you
could dissent within the party.

00:06:31.527 --> 00:06:33.610
That wasn't my experience
at the time, by the way.

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:35.310
It was the experience for
the victors, but - no,

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:36.185
anyone to the left...

00:06:36.185 --> 00:06:37.765
You wouldn't have
been shouted at.

00:06:37.765 --> 00:06:38.310
Trots.

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:38.890
All the time.

00:06:38.890 --> 00:06:40.980
Used a catch-all term for
anyone vaguely on the left.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:41.522
All the time.

00:06:41.522 --> 00:06:43.320
OK, that was not my experience.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:45.355
There was a much
more open discourse.

00:06:45.355 --> 00:06:46.220
But it's not...

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:48.130
because you were on
the winning side.

00:06:48.130 --> 00:06:48.630
No.

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:49.365
Of course you didn't get...

00:06:49.365 --> 00:06:50.190
No, I just...

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:51.240
You'd won.

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.866
Your side won and the left
fell ostracised and besieged.

00:06:53.866 --> 00:06:56.700
Well, can I just say, this
left-right really irritates me.

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:59.400
Really irritateS,
it does irritate me.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:00.825
Because you know,
here you are...

00:07:00.825 --> 00:07:02.640
There's a left and right
flank of the Labour party.

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:03.070
That's just...

00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:03.800
I mean, people on the...

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:05.633
Yeah, but I do not
understand what it means.

00:07:05.633 --> 00:07:07.680
Because I think your
definition of left,

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:10.972
which is around big
state, nationalisation...

00:07:10.972 --> 00:07:11.490
Democratic.

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:12.166
Democratic ...

00:07:12.166 --> 00:07:13.260
No, it was...

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:14.238
you're joking.

00:07:14.238 --> 00:07:15.030
No, I'm not joking.

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:17.560
I've just been through
reselection, Owen, OK?

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:18.060
Right.

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:19.380
In my party.

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:23.730
The whole triggering of
that process was by the few,

00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:25.590
to defeat the will of the many.

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:28.560
So all this stuff about
we're fantastic, democratic.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:30.239
The Labour party
now is the most...

00:07:30.239 --> 00:07:31.156
You won the selection.

00:07:31.156 --> 00:07:35.152
...intolerant,
centralised, bullying.

00:07:35.152 --> 00:07:36.735
It's a terrible,
terrible institution.

00:07:36.735 --> 00:07:37.050
OK.

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:38.830
I obviously strongly
disagree with that.

00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:40.920
And I don't think
democracy is bullying.

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:41.895
You won your selection.

00:07:41.895 --> 00:07:42.367
It isn't democracy.

00:07:42.367 --> 00:07:44.010
You won your selection
fair and square

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:45.425
because the many voted for you.

00:07:45.425 --> 00:07:48.150
But the fact I went
into it in the run-up,

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:49.770
is completely crazy.

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:51.792
It distracted me
from what I should

00:07:51.792 --> 00:07:54.000
have been doing, because it
was obvious we were going

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:55.850
to get a general
election, and I should

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:57.420
have been out there
in the community

00:07:57.420 --> 00:07:59.087
reconnecting with my voters.

00:07:59.087 --> 00:07:59.670
Can I ask you?

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:02.100
This, I think,
because here we are.

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:04.200
We are from different
wings of the Labour family,

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:05.370
let's just put it that way.

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:06.450
But it's interesting
we're having

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:08.158
this argument, or
discussion, or whatever

00:08:08.158 --> 00:08:10.440
you want to call
it, when actually,

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:13.260
if we're honest
and humble, neither

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:16.200
of our brands of politics have
any easy answers at the moment.

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:19.230
And the reason I say that is,
you talk about the 90s, Blair

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:20.550
and Brown and so on.

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:22.890
A period before the
financial crash.

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:24.660
A period of rising
living standards.

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:26.640
There was a financial
bubble at the time,

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:29.580
which seemed to be generating
ever rising living standards.

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:32.695
The problem is at the moment,
there is a general crisis...

00:08:32.695 --> 00:08:33.195
Yeah.

00:08:33.195 --> 00:08:36.690
...of social democracy, and
there is nowhere either of us

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:39.809
can point to on a
map and say, aha,

00:08:39.809 --> 00:08:42.330
here's where my brand
of politics has truly,

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:43.200
truly flourishing.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:44.942
Because Clinton was
defeated by Trump.

00:08:44.942 --> 00:08:45.900
We're agreeing on this.

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:47.430
But that's why I look
at this mess now.

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:48.420
I mean, what's the
answer to that?

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:49.170
I don't have one.

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:50.460
I don't have on either.

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:53.250
I mean that - I agree with
you about the challenge.

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:54.960
I think it's really tough.

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:57.780
I think it's really,
really tough how you build

00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:00.690
on the eternal values, which I
hope you and I do agree about,

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:04.080
and challenges, which I think
lead to massive insecurity,

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:06.765
so the changing world of
work and artificial...

00:09:06.765 --> 00:09:07.732
Climate crisis.

00:09:07.732 --> 00:09:09.070
Climate crisis.

00:09:09.070 --> 00:09:12.273
All those are really tough
challenges for the left.

00:09:12.273 --> 00:09:12.815
But that's...

00:09:12.815 --> 00:09:13.990
And that, I would
love to talk about.

00:09:13.990 --> 00:09:16.532
That in fact, on that, that's
what I'd be fascinated to hear.

00:09:16.532 --> 00:09:19.740
Because I think even some
more perceptive thinkers

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:22.600
on your flank of
the Labour party.

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:23.730
Don't call me a flank.

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:24.345
I get...

00:09:24.345 --> 00:09:26.130
Well I can't call
you from the right,

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:27.797
because you get even
angrier about that.

00:09:27.797 --> 00:09:31.260
Is that wing, was politically
and intellectually

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:32.916
exhausted in 2015.

00:09:32.916 --> 00:09:33.435
It was.

00:09:33.435 --> 00:09:35.490
What I would say is the
challenge facing anyone

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:36.960
who does become leader is huge.

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:39.750
With a culture war which has
opened up in British society,

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:43.110
which Brexit both kind of
encouraged, but also reflected,

00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:46.440
and has wreaked havoc in
Labour's electoral coalition.

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:48.900
And I don't think anyone, from
whatever wing of the Labour

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:53.250
party, has an easy answer, to
how they bring younger people

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:55.380
from - who are
economically insecure -

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:57.630
and have very strong
progressive values,

00:09:57.630 --> 00:09:59.130
which they think
are under attack,

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:02.830
with older people who rightly
have their triple-lock pensions

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:05.310
protected, home
ownership's gone up,

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:07.560
and they feel quite
socially conservative.

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:09.233
And how Labour wins
some of those over,

00:10:09.233 --> 00:10:11.400
and keeps their younger
voters, is a real challenge.

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:14.805
And I think all of us be
humble about discussing how

00:10:14.805 --> 00:10:15.305
difficult...

00:10:15.305 --> 00:10:17.540
You know, I totally
agree with that analysis,

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:19.130
and I just hope
that there can be

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:24.620
a civilised, non-blaming debate
about what are really, really

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:25.560
tough issues.

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:28.470
These are probably the
toughest philosophical,

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:30.770
political issues,
that I have confronted

00:10:30.770 --> 00:10:32.790
in my very long political life.

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:39.480
Hi, It's Margaret Hodge.

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:41.820
Listen, I'm ringing
you about next Thursday

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:46.140
when we've got the meeting to
do the nomination for leader.

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:47.580
I mean, this is
really important,

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:48.622
getting the right leader.

00:10:48.622 --> 00:10:52.140
You know, if we want to bring
the party back to sensible

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:53.900
Labour politics, you know.

00:10:53.900 --> 00:10:57.180
OK, thanks so, so much,
and see you on Thursday.

00:10:57.180 --> 00:10:57.810
Bye.

00:10:57.810 --> 00:10:58.830
Bye.

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:00.860
Well, I don't know
where Sienna is, but...

00:11:00.860 --> 00:11:02.055
I'm going to go and
try and find her.

00:11:02.055 --> 00:11:02.555
Are you?

00:11:02.555 --> 00:11:04.600
Good, because otherwise
it can be chaos.

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:07.262
We're going to be here
forever otherwise, Cameron.

00:11:07.262 --> 00:11:08.970
I really believe that
the Corbyn project,

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:13.590
the socialist manifesto, the
return to true Labour values,

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:16.073
has actually been a
really good success.

00:11:16.073 --> 00:11:16.990
Would you like some...

00:11:16.990 --> 00:11:18.420
No, thank you very much, indeed.

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:21.180
The last thing I want
is more, more Corbyn.

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:26.140
You should be
thinking about it too.

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:26.670
You think...

00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:30.600
Well, you said the last time
you wanted to win, didn't you?

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:35.130
Are you a member of
the Labour party?

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:35.630
Are you?

00:11:38.660 --> 00:11:41.460
Should we just count
them up now and close it?

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:42.630
Um.

00:11:42.630 --> 00:11:45.030
Fingers crossed.

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:50.472
15, 16, 27, 28.

00:11:50.472 --> 00:11:54.387
Two short of the required
number to get 50 per cent.

00:11:54.387 --> 00:11:55.595
So did Emily...

00:11:55.595 --> 00:11:58.380
...candidate, and
we go on eliminated.

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:03.170
The result is - Keir Starmer
is number one, so he won.

00:12:03.170 --> 00:12:03.670
So...

00:12:06.975 --> 00:12:12.330
The continuity candidate
didn't get anywhere tonight.

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:14.100
And I've always
said well, you know,

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:16.730
there are arguments
for Lisa and for Keir.

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:18.350
I would have marginally
gone for Lisa,

00:12:18.350 --> 00:12:21.540
but I'm absolutely
delighted that we've

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:22.840
got a sensible candidate.

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:28.085
A sensible candidate
from this constituency.

00:12:28.085 --> 00:12:30.300
I don't think you can
just sort of say, oh well,

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:33.240
let's assume Corbyn isn't gone.

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:36.300
Because the party is...

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:38.993
I think the party has
changed fundamentally.

00:12:38.993 --> 00:12:41.160
You can see this in the
leadership election going on

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:41.670
now.

00:12:41.670 --> 00:12:44.790
None of them can quite
bring themselves to say

00:12:44.790 --> 00:12:46.920
that Corbynism was a disaster.

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:49.470
Jeremy is this sort
of sainted figure.

00:12:49.470 --> 00:12:50.970
I mean, I agree
with you about that.

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:54.270
I so agree with you, because
there's a central dishonesty

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:56.455
in the non-Corbyn candidates.

00:12:56.455 --> 00:12:56.955
Yeah.

00:12:56.955 --> 00:12:57.930
You know.

00:12:57.930 --> 00:13:00.210
And if you compare
that to Kinnock,

00:13:00.210 --> 00:13:03.510
Kinnock when he fought for the
leadership of the Labour party

00:13:03.510 --> 00:13:07.350
in the 80s, came from the left
but he was completely honest

00:13:07.350 --> 00:13:10.012
about the sort of reform
that had to be undertaken.

00:13:10.012 --> 00:13:14.500
I mean, Neil was very good a
kind of, political management.

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:18.750
But I had a very
interesting exchange

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:22.400
with Keir when he launched
his leadership video.

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:25.290
And it was you know,
miners' strike and poll tax.

00:13:25.290 --> 00:13:28.140
And I sent him a message saying,
I'm really pleased that I gave

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:32.370
most of my adult life to
trying to help the Labour party

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:34.020
into power.

00:13:34.020 --> 00:13:38.940
Three terms in office, so that
a future leader could launch his

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:42.240
campaign, where the only
mentions of that period

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:46.770
of Labour in office were the
Iraq war and cutting asylum

00:13:46.770 --> 00:13:47.730
seekers' benefits.

00:13:47.730 --> 00:13:50.760
And it's just playing into
that Corbynist agenda.

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:53.010
So he then sent me a message
back and said, yeah well,

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:54.480
I'm doing this new thing
now where I'm saying,

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:56.010
we shouldn't trash
the last four years,

00:13:56.010 --> 00:13:58.093
and we shouldn't trash the
last Labour government.

00:13:58.093 --> 00:13:59.910
Like they recall.

00:13:59.910 --> 00:14:03.810
The last four years, I
get why he can't say it,

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:05.630
but we have to be
honest with people,

00:14:05.630 --> 00:14:10.260
it's been a fucking disaster
for the Labour party.

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:12.900
The elections alone
were a disaster.

00:14:12.900 --> 00:14:15.667
I think too much of our
politics is about saying, oh,

00:14:15.667 --> 00:14:18.000
this is what people want to
hear, let's say that to them

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:19.240
and play it back to them.

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:19.740
So when...

00:14:19.740 --> 00:14:21.205
You did a bit of
that in the 80s.

00:14:21.205 --> 00:14:22.150
I did a bit of it.

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:22.995
You did a bit.

00:14:22.995 --> 00:14:26.400
The message can be
simple, but it's

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:29.400
got to be underpinned by
something that is real.

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:33.520
Real policy, real stuff,
real choice, real decision.

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:36.420
If you look at our last
election, Corbyn or Johnson.

00:14:36.420 --> 00:14:39.030
The country was
repelled by the choice,

00:14:39.030 --> 00:14:41.673
but in the end realised well,
we have to make a... we have

00:14:41.673 --> 00:14:42.590
to choose one of them.

00:14:42.590 --> 00:14:44.340
One of them is going
to be prime minister.

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:45.315
Who's it going to be?

00:14:45.315 --> 00:14:46.570
But go beyond that.

00:14:46.570 --> 00:14:48.000
So we got a charismatic leader.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:51.300
I feel more confident than
you do that we're going to get

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:53.860
a change of leader and that
will try and put us back...

00:14:53.860 --> 00:14:54.360
Oh, we are.

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:55.955
There's definitely going
to be a change of leader.

00:14:55.955 --> 00:14:57.625
Well, it's not going to
be Rebecca Long-Bailey.

00:14:57.625 --> 00:14:58.110
Right.

00:14:58.110 --> 00:14:58.610
OK.

00:14:58.610 --> 00:14:59.262
It's my view.

00:14:59.262 --> 00:15:00.720
Well, if it is
Rebecca Long-Bailey,

00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:01.680
I think that's the end.

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:02.925
I agree.

00:15:02.925 --> 00:15:04.410
I think we might
as well go home.

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:08.700
Some of the values that
now inform people's voting

00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:11.740
decisions are really
hard for us to tackle.

00:15:11.740 --> 00:15:15.510
So let's assume we've got
a charismatic leader who

00:15:15.510 --> 00:15:17.370
can connect to people.

00:15:17.370 --> 00:15:20.640
How are they going to tackle
the issues of immigration?

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:22.200
Which is really tough.

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:24.180
Which we have to think
about, we didn't do.

00:15:24.180 --> 00:15:26.430
We didn't really get
to the bottom of.

00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:30.600
And how are they going to tackle
tax and spend in an environment

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:32.250
where people don't want that?

00:15:32.250 --> 00:15:35.880
How can I create a more equal
society, out of this brief

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:37.520
that I've now been given.

00:15:37.520 --> 00:15:42.180
This is why I think
it goes beyond what...

00:15:42.180 --> 00:15:45.720
Corbynism goes beyond who
actually becomes the leader.

00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:49.110
You know, look, I can't stand
Johnson on many, many levels,

00:15:49.110 --> 00:15:54.960
but one of the reasons he won is
that, he gave a kind of, albeit

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:58.020
riddled with lies and all
that, he gave a sense of kind

00:15:58.020 --> 00:15:59.470
of, optimism to people.

00:15:59.470 --> 00:16:00.675
Yeah, and hope.

00:16:00.675 --> 00:16:03.450
So I would say, take
all of those issues

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:06.240
you mentioned, climate
change being the biggest.

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:11.390
I think a leader who came
out now and was truthful

00:16:11.390 --> 00:16:14.030
about the consequences
of climate change,

00:16:14.030 --> 00:16:16.820
and really led on them,
and really fought on them,

00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:18.710
gives themselves
a fighting chance.

00:16:18.710 --> 00:16:21.020
The other thing that's
changed I think -

00:16:21.020 --> 00:16:24.246
the left-right arguments are
less straightforward than they

00:16:24.246 --> 00:16:24.746
were.

00:16:24.746 --> 00:16:26.030
But I hate this term left-right.

00:16:26.030 --> 00:16:26.456
I mean...

00:16:26.456 --> 00:16:27.456
But we're trapped in it.

00:16:27.456 --> 00:16:30.110
Don't underestimate
the impact of Trump

00:16:30.110 --> 00:16:34.050
in framing the debate
right around the world.

00:16:34.050 --> 00:16:37.200
The whole kind of nationalist,
populist language,

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:37.700
Yeah.

00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:40.760
That is easy to
articulate, it's easier

00:16:40.760 --> 00:16:43.430
to get people to resonate with.

00:16:43.430 --> 00:16:46.520
So but the way you
frame the debate

00:16:46.520 --> 00:16:50.740
is to decide that is the
debate I'm going to frame.

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:55.820
Now, at the moment, I feel
that it's being framed within

00:16:55.820 --> 00:16:57.575
this...

00:16:57.575 --> 00:16:59.480
Historical analysis
of the Labour party.

00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:00.567
Pretty much, yeah.

00:17:00.567 --> 00:17:01.265
Terrible.

00:17:01.265 --> 00:17:01.550
Yeah.

00:17:01.550 --> 00:17:03.175
The other thing that
fills me with hope

00:17:03.175 --> 00:17:04.940
is the leader we
elect now is unlikely

00:17:04.940 --> 00:17:07.490
ever to become prime minister,
given the mountain we

00:17:07.490 --> 00:17:08.869
have to climb back.

00:17:08.869 --> 00:17:10.657
I don't think you
should say that.

00:17:10.657 --> 00:17:12.115
You don't think we
should say that?

00:17:12.115 --> 00:17:14.640
You think it's so volatile
that we can't bring it back...

00:17:14.640 --> 00:17:15.140
Yeah.

00:17:15.140 --> 00:17:17.465
I think things are so volatile,
things move so quickly.

00:17:17.465 --> 00:17:18.800
I think some of the most...

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:22.089
That is... that will definitely
be the case if the leader that

00:17:22.089 --> 00:17:24.215
the Labour party
elects plays safe.

00:17:24.215 --> 00:17:25.760
So if I were leader
tomorrow, what

00:17:25.760 --> 00:17:30.200
would I do to create
something true to my lasting

00:17:30.200 --> 00:17:33.750
values, relevant to today,
that gets me a majority?

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:34.250
OK.

00:17:34.250 --> 00:17:36.230
Well neither you and
I would be elected,

00:17:36.230 --> 00:17:38.630
because I think we
would both start

00:17:38.630 --> 00:17:40.670
from a position
of saying, we have

00:17:40.670 --> 00:17:44.740
to be absolutely honest about
how we've got to this position.

00:17:44.740 --> 00:17:47.420
And we have to be absolutely
honest about the challenges we

00:17:47.420 --> 00:17:49.410
face to get back.

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:55.070
So I would build it on
understanding and accepting

00:17:55.070 --> 00:18:00.710
that the right, the
nationalist, populist right,

00:18:00.710 --> 00:18:04.160
is going to be very
difficult to beat.

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:10.640
And then saying they will
not be beaten by aping them.

00:18:10.640 --> 00:18:15.110
They won't be beaten by
beating them halfway.

00:18:15.110 --> 00:18:19.140
They have to be beaten by
a bigger better, bolder,

00:18:19.140 --> 00:18:21.805
more optimistic vision
about what Britain can be.

00:18:21.805 --> 00:18:23.430
This idea that, you
know, that it's all

00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:25.700
got to be about getting
the right northerner,

00:18:25.700 --> 00:18:28.070
as a response to the
north just putting

00:18:28.070 --> 00:18:30.770
a bloody old Etonian in power.

00:18:30.770 --> 00:18:32.340
They won the referendum.

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:34.250
They won the election.

00:18:34.250 --> 00:18:36.380
They have a lot more
power than they did.

00:18:36.380 --> 00:18:38.960
Now, the difference, I think,
between when I was with Tony,

00:18:38.960 --> 00:18:41.090
and Cummings now
with Johnson, I never

00:18:41.090 --> 00:18:42.520
really had an agenda of my own.

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:45.020
I had things I agreed with, and
things that I disagree with,

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:48.470
but I kind of, I didn't see
my position as been there

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:49.710
for my agenda.

00:18:49.710 --> 00:18:50.210
Go on.

00:18:50.210 --> 00:18:51.380
You're back in the Labour party.

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:52.640
You've got to vote Alastair.

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:54.435
How would you use it
for the leadership?

00:18:54.435 --> 00:18:56.060
Well, I'm not back
in the Labour party.

00:18:56.060 --> 00:18:57.222
Yeah, but assume.

00:18:57.222 --> 00:18:57.722
Eh.

00:19:02.006 --> 00:19:03.960
I think probably Keir.

00:19:06.810 --> 00:19:08.115
Probably Keir.

00:19:08.115 --> 00:19:10.530
And do you think he
can turn it around?

00:19:10.530 --> 00:19:13.680
I really don't know,
because I think it's...

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:16.420
I think a lot of these
top jobs in politics,

00:19:16.420 --> 00:19:20.235
I don't think you really
know until they get to them.

00:19:20.235 --> 00:19:23.725
My fear is that the only
way he can turn it around

00:19:23.725 --> 00:19:26.100
is by doing something different
from what he's telling us

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:27.045
he's going to do now.

00:19:27.045 --> 00:19:29.212
I think the short answer
is, I don't think any of us

00:19:29.212 --> 00:19:31.560
know yet, whether
they've got it in them.

00:19:31.560 --> 00:19:34.200
I've taken over the chair of
the, the parliamentary chair

00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:35.460
of the Jewish Labour movement.

00:19:35.460 --> 00:19:39.330
Really, because everybody
else who's done has gone.

00:19:39.330 --> 00:19:41.370
Scary, but it's true.

00:19:41.370 --> 00:19:44.130
And there are very,
very few Jews now,

00:19:44.130 --> 00:19:45.625
on the Labour side
in parliament.

00:19:45.625 --> 00:19:47.000
And probably I'm
the only one who

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:48.900
is willing to put my
head above the parapet,

00:19:48.900 --> 00:19:50.810
around my Jewish identity.

00:19:50.810 --> 00:19:53.640
And also it's symbolic of what
the Labour party ought to be

00:19:53.640 --> 00:19:54.630
all about.

00:19:54.630 --> 00:19:57.270
How we're going to build
that confidence quickly?

00:19:57.270 --> 00:19:59.890
It was really uncomfortable
for me during the election,

00:19:59.890 --> 00:20:03.310
going out and trying to persuade
Jewish people to vote Labour.

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:06.030
Oh my God, there's going to
be absolutely loads of people,

00:20:06.030 --> 00:20:07.400
aren't there?

00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:08.780
700 people.

00:20:08.780 --> 00:20:14.176
Jews come in all different
shapes and sizes.

00:20:14.176 --> 00:20:16.930
... but I'm the only
one whose fucking left.

00:20:16.930 --> 00:20:18.285
Are they not going to come in?

00:20:18.285 --> 00:20:18.785
No.

00:20:18.785 --> 00:20:20.390
They're just going to ...

00:20:20.390 --> 00:20:23.255
I was going to ask
some questions.

00:20:23.255 --> 00:20:25.380
That - we're going to bring
them on a - we're going

00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:26.940
to bring them all on together.

00:20:26.940 --> 00:20:29.160
It's fine, but we
need to begin now.

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:30.645
Otherwise, we're going to...

00:20:30.645 --> 00:20:31.145
OK.

00:20:37.335 --> 00:20:40.180
Thanks Mike, and thanks for
giving me the opportunity

00:20:40.180 --> 00:20:45.820
to say a few words, because I am
virtually the last Jewish woman

00:20:45.820 --> 00:20:47.900
Labour MP left standing.

00:20:47.900 --> 00:20:52.210
Rebecca, we are going to take
them in once Adrian's finished.

00:20:52.210 --> 00:20:54.670
If you look at after
the war and before,

00:20:54.670 --> 00:21:01.870
to the mid '70s, most Jewish MPs
were Labour MPs because being

00:21:01.870 --> 00:21:05.440
in the Labour party was
the natural home for Jews.

00:21:05.440 --> 00:21:07.690
Sadly, we're not in
that position today.

00:21:10.380 --> 00:21:11.470
I hope the candidates...

00:21:11.470 --> 00:21:14.137
I was hoping they'd be in when I
said all this, but they're not.

00:21:14.137 --> 00:21:16.660
So maybe some of you can ask
them the questions afterwards.

00:21:16.660 --> 00:21:19.990
I think for all of us, I felt
at the last four years, for me,

00:21:19.990 --> 00:21:22.960
have been the most
miserable, challenging,

00:21:22.960 --> 00:21:26.650
and lonely years in my
nearly 60 years of membership

00:21:26.650 --> 00:21:28.170
of the Labour party.

00:21:28.170 --> 00:21:33.820
And I know that's what every Jew
sitting here tonight feels too.

00:21:33.820 --> 00:21:37.210
The history of the last
four years is being rapidly

00:21:37.210 --> 00:21:40.870
rewritten by those who
are seeking our support,

00:21:40.870 --> 00:21:45.400
and my commitment to you is I
will not cease fighting until

00:21:45.400 --> 00:21:49.120
we are clear that we have
eradicated anti-Semitism from

00:21:49.120 --> 00:21:51.880
the soul of our
great Labour party.

00:21:59.820 --> 00:22:02.130
I know all of these
individuals extremely well,

00:22:02.130 --> 00:22:05.800
and they're all thoroughly
nice, thoroughly decent people.

00:22:05.800 --> 00:22:07.310
And they're doing
a very tough job,

00:22:07.310 --> 00:22:10.262
and we have to treat
them with respect.

00:22:10.262 --> 00:22:11.720
Because you know,
we're just bloody

00:22:11.720 --> 00:22:14.270
lucky to have people in
this country prepared

00:22:14.270 --> 00:22:18.560
to put their necks on the
chopping block for our welfare.

00:22:21.195 --> 00:22:26.605
Becky, did you stand up at
any point, and call it out?

00:22:26.605 --> 00:22:28.390
I did, but was that enough?

00:22:28.390 --> 00:22:29.140
No.

00:22:29.140 --> 00:22:30.270
It wasn't.

00:22:30.270 --> 00:22:31.060
And why?

00:22:31.060 --> 00:22:34.090
What I did was, I did
speak in shadow cabinet

00:22:34.090 --> 00:22:36.010
about this a few times.

00:22:36.010 --> 00:22:39.052
I believe that you speak
out, and speak up in private,

00:22:39.052 --> 00:22:40.510
and you try and
get something done.

00:22:40.510 --> 00:22:43.690
If you can't get something
done and it's still wrong,

00:22:43.690 --> 00:22:44.890
then you leave.

00:22:44.890 --> 00:22:46.840
And you speak out,
and you speak up,

00:22:46.840 --> 00:22:49.042
and you make sure that
something is done.

00:22:49.042 --> 00:22:52.422
Oh no, I just sat there
thinking, I've got to shut up.

00:22:52.422 --> 00:22:53.380
I mustn't say anything.

00:22:53.380 --> 00:22:56.530
This was the big fight for the
heart and soul of the Labour

00:22:56.530 --> 00:22:57.400
party.

00:22:57.400 --> 00:23:02.740
If a Corbyn follower
succeeds Corbyn,

00:23:02.740 --> 00:23:07.090
then I think the position
for Jews in the Labour party,

00:23:07.090 --> 00:23:09.850
long-term, is
completely untenable.

00:23:09.850 --> 00:23:10.635
Hi Jess.

00:23:10.635 --> 00:23:11.210
Hi.

00:23:11.210 --> 00:23:11.710
Hi.

00:23:12.972 --> 00:23:14.035
Jeremy Corbyn's office?

00:23:15.490 --> 00:23:19.420
You are a breath of fresh
air, and that's why I'm sure

00:23:19.420 --> 00:23:21.670
you've got a great future
in the Labour party.

00:23:21.670 --> 00:23:24.160
But for me that's why I
wanted you as the leader.

00:23:24.160 --> 00:23:28.000
That you were authentic,
you told it as it is.

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:31.820
That you were brave,
and that you really,

00:23:31.820 --> 00:23:35.020
really could connect
with ordinary people.

00:23:35.020 --> 00:23:37.760
And for me, in all the battles
that I've done through my life,

00:23:37.760 --> 00:23:39.910
maybe the battle
against the BNP,

00:23:39.910 --> 00:23:44.170
I understood from that
the absolute importance

00:23:44.170 --> 00:23:48.550
of being both honest,
but really listening,

00:23:48.550 --> 00:23:50.150
acting, and connecting.

00:23:50.150 --> 00:23:52.150
And that's why you're so
important to the Labour

00:23:52.150 --> 00:23:52.650
movement.

00:23:52.650 --> 00:23:54.310
And will be in the future.

00:23:54.310 --> 00:23:55.180
Well, let's hope so.

00:23:55.180 --> 00:23:59.680
The idea of standing up,
regardless of the struggle,

00:23:59.680 --> 00:24:02.020
regardless of how
difficult something is.

00:24:02.020 --> 00:24:05.720
That sort of courage,
and that sort of bravery,

00:24:05.720 --> 00:24:08.920
it has felt sometimes
as if that has just no

00:24:08.920 --> 00:24:11.570
longer been part of the battle.

00:24:11.570 --> 00:24:14.440
It was that we had to you
know agree with the crowd,

00:24:14.440 --> 00:24:16.760
regardless of what
the crowd felt.

00:24:16.760 --> 00:24:17.460
Yeah.

00:24:17.460 --> 00:24:19.335
Truly, the other thing
I really feel strongly

00:24:19.335 --> 00:24:20.680
is, in the anti-Semitism fight.

00:24:20.680 --> 00:24:21.650
Oh gosh, yeah.

00:24:21.650 --> 00:24:23.140
You know, it was the women.

00:24:23.140 --> 00:24:24.015
It was totally...

00:24:24.015 --> 00:24:26.350
And then, I will
never forget you.

00:24:26.350 --> 00:24:29.260
Just after Luciana Berger had
decided to leave the Labour

00:24:29.260 --> 00:24:33.262
party, and you rushed
over to sit behind her.

00:24:33.262 --> 00:24:33.970
Behind her, yeah.

00:24:33.970 --> 00:24:36.250
So you showed some
solidarity with her.

00:24:36.250 --> 00:24:37.840
It's always been the women.

00:24:37.840 --> 00:24:40.390
And then you demonstrated
in that very clear way,

00:24:40.390 --> 00:24:44.353
that even though she felt forced
out she was still your sister.

00:24:44.353 --> 00:24:45.220
Of course she will.

00:24:45.220 --> 00:24:47.440
Labour women standing
shoulder to shoulder,

00:24:47.440 --> 00:24:53.140
is the reason that I, no matter
how hard things get within

00:24:53.140 --> 00:24:56.740
the Labour party, the
reason I cannot walk away is

00:24:56.740 --> 00:24:59.980
because my life was genuinely
changed by the fact that loads

00:24:59.980 --> 00:25:03.030
of Labour women came
in here in 1997.

00:25:03.030 --> 00:25:04.960
They never ever
forgot to remember me,

00:25:04.960 --> 00:25:06.786
people like me in the community.

00:25:06.786 --> 00:25:07.490
Sure start.

00:25:07.490 --> 00:25:07.990
Exactly.

00:25:07.990 --> 00:25:10.797
Your sure start, your tax
credit, your childcare.

00:25:10.797 --> 00:25:13.255
I will never, ever be able to
pay back the debt of the fact

00:25:13.255 --> 00:25:15.730
that my children have
more secure lives

00:25:15.730 --> 00:25:19.030
than I would have had they been
born under a Tory government.

00:25:19.030 --> 00:25:21.903
And so the idea that has been
presented to us for the past

00:25:21.903 --> 00:25:24.070
four or five years, whether
it's over anti-Semitism,

00:25:24.070 --> 00:25:26.570
whether it's over women
within the Labour movement,

00:25:26.570 --> 00:25:28.930
whether it's ever sexual
harassment - they've been like,

00:25:28.930 --> 00:25:30.580
you're disloyal for struggling.

00:25:30.580 --> 00:25:32.860
And I'm like, no, I'm here
to help the Labour party.

00:25:32.860 --> 00:25:35.560
I didn't do it for
me, I did it because I

00:25:35.560 --> 00:25:38.145
believe it's the right
thing for our movement.

00:25:38.145 --> 00:25:40.565
And actually, I joined
the Labour party...

00:25:40.565 --> 00:25:42.100
Because it's a struggle?

00:25:42.100 --> 00:25:42.690
To fight it.

00:25:42.690 --> 00:25:43.360
Fight.

00:25:43.360 --> 00:25:45.970
How do you think you
square the circle

00:25:45.970 --> 00:25:49.390
between the traditional
working class

00:25:49.390 --> 00:25:53.230
support that we used to have,
and we want to get back,

00:25:53.230 --> 00:25:56.980
and the more middle class,
liberal support that comes out

00:25:56.980 --> 00:25:58.810
of the city?

00:25:58.810 --> 00:26:01.180
It's absolutely not my
experience of working class

00:26:01.180 --> 00:26:03.220
communities in the
Midlands and the north,

00:26:03.220 --> 00:26:06.815
that they all hate immigrants,
or that they all...

00:26:06.815 --> 00:26:07.940
Hate feminists.

00:26:07.940 --> 00:26:11.110
The Labour party being the
party of the self-interest

00:26:11.110 --> 00:26:14.090
of the masses has been lost,
because we stopped being able

00:26:14.090 --> 00:26:15.340
to say the word self-interest.

00:26:15.340 --> 00:26:17.902
And my nan and granddad -
my great-grandfather was one

00:26:17.902 --> 00:26:20.110
of the people who set up
the independent Labour party

00:26:20.110 --> 00:26:23.320
in Birmingham, and it
wasn't because he was...

00:26:23.320 --> 00:26:26.440
had big ideas about the way
that the world should be.

00:26:26.440 --> 00:26:29.590
It was because he
lived in a slum

00:26:29.590 --> 00:26:32.200
and he didn't want to
live in a slum anymore.

00:26:32.200 --> 00:26:33.370
He had self-interest.

00:26:33.370 --> 00:26:35.560
And my nan and
granddad's politics

00:26:35.560 --> 00:26:40.630
was about moving out of the
slum and getting a decent wage.

00:26:40.630 --> 00:26:43.240
It was self-interested.

00:26:43.240 --> 00:26:46.150
It wasn't so that
everybody has enough.

00:26:46.150 --> 00:26:48.130
It was for them
and their families,

00:26:48.130 --> 00:26:49.660
to be able to do all right.

00:26:49.660 --> 00:26:52.880
And then their children, the
baby boomers, my parents,

00:26:52.880 --> 00:26:55.810
they had enough to care about
everybody having enough,

00:26:55.810 --> 00:26:58.420
and then it became an
ideological movement, rather

00:26:58.420 --> 00:27:00.470
than a self-interested one.

00:27:00.470 --> 00:27:03.760
But there's absolutely no
reason why you cannot square

00:27:03.760 --> 00:27:06.010
ideologically wanting
everybody to have enough,

00:27:06.010 --> 00:27:08.650
with the idea that you and your
family feel like we haven't got

00:27:08.650 --> 00:27:10.210
enough and need it.

00:27:10.210 --> 00:27:11.440
Those two things.

00:27:11.440 --> 00:27:14.770
My nan and my mum, they're
cut from the same cloth,

00:27:14.770 --> 00:27:18.280
they were the same, exactly
the same sort of person.

00:27:18.280 --> 00:27:21.280
They both would have voted
Labour until they died,

00:27:21.280 --> 00:27:24.160
and it was rooted in something
completely separate for both

00:27:24.160 --> 00:27:24.880
of them.

00:27:24.880 --> 00:27:26.840
That is the coalition.

00:27:26.840 --> 00:27:29.140
I think we think it's a
harder job than it is,

00:27:29.140 --> 00:27:32.560
to make people in Bassettlaw
feel the same way as people

00:27:32.560 --> 00:27:33.330
in Brighton.

00:27:33.330 --> 00:27:35.200
They essentially
do feel the same.

00:27:35.200 --> 00:27:37.120
I feel like this should
now be an opportunity

00:27:37.120 --> 00:27:39.100
to look up to the country
a little bit more.

00:27:39.100 --> 00:27:41.140
To look up from the script.

00:27:41.140 --> 00:27:42.640
I don't feel that
there's any sense

00:27:42.640 --> 00:27:45.700
that world leaders
are getting together

00:27:45.700 --> 00:27:47.695
to deal with the pandemic.

00:27:47.695 --> 00:27:51.235
I don't quite know how
he's going to rise to that.

00:27:51.235 --> 00:27:53.110
I think that the
connecting with the country

00:27:53.110 --> 00:27:54.760
is vitally important.

00:27:54.760 --> 00:27:57.660
Now, I think that he will
inevitably get a bounce.

00:27:57.660 --> 00:28:02.190
Also, I hate this, because it is
got so much sort of, horrible,

00:28:02.190 --> 00:28:05.490
sexist connotations - he
looks the part, doesn't he?

00:28:05.490 --> 00:28:06.677
I hate that.

00:28:06.677 --> 00:28:08.260
The number of people
who said to me...

00:28:08.260 --> 00:28:09.175
I hate it too.

00:28:09.175 --> 00:28:10.800
The number of people
who've said to me,

00:28:10.800 --> 00:28:12.870
you've got to vote
for Keir, because just

00:28:12.870 --> 00:28:16.800
think of if Boris implodes,
you trust Keir opposite you.

00:28:16.800 --> 00:28:18.345
Why wouldn't you trust a woman?

00:28:18.345 --> 00:28:18.845
Well.

00:28:18.845 --> 00:28:20.210
Well, quite.

00:28:20.210 --> 00:28:21.420
Absolutely.

00:28:21.420 --> 00:28:22.890
But he's got good
hair, doesn't he?

00:28:22.890 --> 00:28:24.098
I mean, he has got good hair.

00:28:24.098 --> 00:28:26.490
My dad is always
like, lovely hair.

00:28:26.490 --> 00:28:29.640
He's ticked that box,
but that is a tiny box

00:28:29.640 --> 00:28:31.035
of connecting with the public.

00:28:31.035 --> 00:28:32.970
I mean, I think we
can all understand

00:28:32.970 --> 00:28:34.590
that the candidates
have got to build

00:28:34.590 --> 00:28:36.030
a majority within the party.

00:28:36.030 --> 00:28:36.870
Right?

00:28:36.870 --> 00:28:37.495
Oh, absolutely.

00:28:37.495 --> 00:28:39.900
But once that election
is over they've

00:28:39.900 --> 00:28:44.040
got to turn to the public,
and they've got to be bold.

00:28:44.040 --> 00:28:46.500
And that means not
sticking to what's happened

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:47.520
in the last five years.

00:28:47.520 --> 00:28:49.740
It means breaking from that.

00:28:49.740 --> 00:28:51.190
And last 20 years.

00:28:51.190 --> 00:28:52.630
You have to think of...

00:28:52.630 --> 00:28:53.220
Except.

00:28:53.220 --> 00:28:55.480
I agree with that, but
the thing that really,

00:28:55.480 --> 00:28:56.570
really pisses me off...

00:28:56.570 --> 00:28:58.362
Is slagging off the
last Labour government.

00:28:58.862 --> 00:29:00.960
Why would you slag off
your record in office

00:29:00.960 --> 00:29:04.162
when the government's degrading
it all brilliantly by itself?

00:29:04.162 --> 00:29:05.870
In the face of a Boris
Johnson government

00:29:05.870 --> 00:29:08.470
- remember, he's got
a majority of 80,

00:29:08.470 --> 00:29:10.470
and is he doing
anything with it?

00:29:10.470 --> 00:29:13.810
I mean, if I had a majority
of 80 Margaret, just think.

00:29:13.810 --> 00:29:15.180
Just even just one thing.

00:29:15.180 --> 00:29:17.920
You'd be like, well OK, we're
going to end child poverty.

00:29:17.920 --> 00:29:19.800
We're going to sort
the social care crisis.

00:29:19.800 --> 00:29:22.050
You would just be like
I'd be like giddy,

00:29:22.050 --> 00:29:24.022
like a child in a sweet shop.

00:29:24.022 --> 00:29:26.340
So Jess, it may have been
a bit too early this time,

00:29:26.340 --> 00:29:27.798
or it may have been
the wrong time,

00:29:27.798 --> 00:29:30.780
but I want you to stand
again for the Labour party

00:29:30.780 --> 00:29:31.430
leadership.

00:29:31.430 --> 00:29:33.805
You've got to promise me you're
going to have a go at it.

00:29:33.805 --> 00:29:36.590
I mean, I promise you
that I will have a go.

00:29:36.590 --> 00:29:42.270
But regardless, I promise you
that I will always try and make

00:29:42.270 --> 00:29:45.300
sure that I have the biggest
role that I can have in making

00:29:45.300 --> 00:29:46.830
the Labour party
the best it can be.

00:29:47.700 --> 00:29:48.570
Thank you.

00:29:48.570 --> 00:29:49.380
You're welcome.

00:29:49.380 --> 00:29:50.910
Thank you.

00:29:50.910 --> 00:29:54.830
I did say to people before
the election hang on in there

00:29:54.830 --> 00:29:57.780
because the battle after the
election will be the most

00:29:57.780 --> 00:30:01.230
important battle in the
life of the Labour party.

00:30:01.230 --> 00:30:07.350
I now think that the way in
which the next leader chooses

00:30:07.350 --> 00:30:11.990
to use that position will
be the most important.

